<h2>Two regimes, one collision</h2>
<p>The MSMED Act, 2006 is a payment-discipline statute: the forty-five day ceiling under Section 15, penal compound interest under Section 16, and the Facilitation Council machinery under Section 18 all exist to extract money from a solvent but slow buyer. The IBC answers a different question — how to distribute the assets of a failing buyer among all claimants fairly. When the two collide, the IBC wins: its moratorium halts MSEFC arbitrations and executions alike, and its distribution scheme replaces the race to recover with a statutory queue.</p>

<h2>The waterfall, as it affects suppliers</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Rank (Section 53 IBC)</th><th>Claim</th><th>Supplier&rsquo;s position</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>CIRP and liquidation costs</td><td>Above the supplier</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2</td><td>Workmen (24 months) and relinquishing secured creditors</td><td>Above the supplier</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>Other employee dues (12 months)</td><td>Above the supplier</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4</td><td>Unsecured financial creditors</td><td>Above the supplier</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>Government dues; secured-creditor enforcement shortfalls</td><td>Above or alongside, depending on composition</td></tr>
  <tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Remaining debts — including operational/trade dues</strong></td><td><strong>The supplier&rsquo;s rung</strong></td></tr>
</table>
<p>The arithmetic explains the empirical pattern: operational creditors in liquidation frequently receive negligible distributions. The realistic recoveries happen earlier — in CIRP through a resolution plan (which must pay operational creditors at least their liquidation value), or before insolvency begins at all.</p>

<h2>Timing strategy: the three windows</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Window one — buyer stressed but solvent.</strong> This is when the MSMED machinery has real teeth. Issue the demand, file the Samadhaan reference, press the Council for the Section 18(5) ninety-day discipline, and convert the receivable into an award while execution is still possible.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Window two — CIRP.</strong> The moratorium stays Council proceedings and execution. File the claim with the resolution professional in the prescribed form, with the award or invoice trail; engage with the process, since a resolution plan is the best remaining outcome. Suppliers who ignore CIRP deadlines risk extinguishment of their claims once a plan is approved — the clean-slate principle leaves late claimants with nothing to enforce.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Window three — liquidation.</strong> Lodge the claim with the liquidator, verify its admission, and assess realistically whether the estate reaches rung six. Where the corporate debtor itself was sold as a going concern, watch the terms for treatment of operational dues.</div>
</div>

<h2>Collateral avenues worth checking</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Personal guarantees of promoters or directors: the moratorium protects the company, not its guarantors, and a guarantee can keep a parallel recovery alive.</p>
  <p>Retention of title and bailment structures: goods supplied on terms that title passes only on payment may support restitution-type claims outside the pure money queue, where documentation is tight.</p>
  <p>Pre-insolvency receipts: amounts actually recovered through MSEFC execution before CIRP commencement are generally safe, subject to the preference/avoidance look-back provisions — another reason early action beats late litigation.</p>
  <p>Section 9 IBC as leverage: for defaults at or above the threshold, a well-founded operational-creditor application often produces payment faster than any other instrument — but it must rest on an undisputed debt.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer sets out the statutory scheme in general terms for information only. Insolvency distributions depend on each estate&rsquo;s facts, and advice should be taken before choosing a recovery route.</div>
